<?php

namespace App\Services\Assistant;

/**
 * What the assistant knows about finding its way around the app: each area, the permission that
 * opens it (as in the sidebar), and how its common tasks are done. Only the areas a user can open
 * are given to the assistant, so it never explains screens they cannot reach.
 */
class AssistantKnowledge
{
    /**
     * [area, path, any of these permissions, what it is and how to use it, and optionally the steps
     * only some roles have: "permission|or another" => text, given only to users with one of them]
     */
    public const AREAS = [
        ['Dashboard', '/dashboard', ['can_view_dashboard_menu'],
            'Overview of sales, receipts, stock and alerts for the company, for the period chosen top right (default Last 30 Days). Cards: Sales = order totals incl. VAT by order date, excluding cancelled; Orders = orders dated in the period; Customers = all customers and how many were added; Net Cash = completed receipts in the period minus approved/paid expenses in the period (salaries, stock bought as expenses, PAYE/NSSF etc. all count), so it goes negative when more is spent than collected. The period picker also has Custom Range: pick From and To dates, then Apply. Each card\'s % badge compares with the same-length period just before: green up = better, red down = worse (a Net Cash going from -170,936 to -952,648 shows red -457%).'],
        ['Customers (CRM)', '/customers', ['can_view_customers_menu'],
            'Customer list. Add one with "Add Customer" (business name, contacts, KRA PIN, region/county/town, payment method; credit customers fill the credit application). A customer is always shown by their business / company name; the contact person only appears where labelled Contact. Click a customer to open their profile: Orders, Invoices, Account (balance, open invoices, unapplied payments, pending cheques), Statement and Notes. The Statement\'s ageing lists each unpaid invoice on its own line (S/No, invoice, invoice date, due date, days past due) with its balance under <30, 30-60, 60-90 or >90 days by its age counted from the invoice date (an invoice 121 days old sits under >90). Days past due is shown separately: the days since its due date, 0 if not yet due (121 days old on 60-day terms is 61 days past due). Finance > Aging also ranges by age. An invoice moves along as it gets older and drops off once paid; with no unpaid invoices the statement has no ageing section at all. The statement PDF never cuts a line: it goes on one page if it stays readable, otherwise it breaks between lines and repeats the column headings. It is worked out as at the To date, after credit notes and receipts (oldest invoice first). Moving an order or invoice to another customer takes its credit notes and any receipt or cheque that paid only that invoice with it; a receipt that also paid other invoices stays with the old customer and goes on their oldest open invoices or their credit, so the moved invoice owes that part. Accountants can delete credit notes. Customers > Accounts lists credit accounts and limits; the GM, admins and accountants approve or reject an account or a credit limit change from the row\'s "..." menu. To delete a customer: the row\'s "..." menu > Delete Customer, then confirm (GM, admins and accountants; any customer). Everything recorded for that customer is deleted with them - orders, invoices, credit notes, receipts, cheques, quotes and collection notes (the box lists how many of each first); stock on their orders goes back, except past orders. It cannot be undone, so it is for clearing test or wrongly entered data. A customer statement is on the profile\'s Statement button; choose the period, then Download (Excel/PDF/CSV) or Print.'],
        ['Quotes', '/sales/quotes', ['can_view_quotes_menu'],
            'Sales > Quotes. "New Quote": pick the customer - or "One-time customer" in the customer list for someone not in the system: type just their name, the payment period (cash, 7-90 days, printed as the payment terms) and pick the Sales Rep (sales team); the quote shows them as "One-time customer". "Convert to Order" on such a quote asks which customer in the system the order goes under (the quote is merged into that customer); register them under Customers first if they are new. Then "Add Product" under the lines (the same search as on orders: type a description, item no. or code like NSPD 001 - matching price codes first, then one line per item at its NSPV price; a full code adds the line at once). Each line\'s code box can be retyped to change the item or use another list\'s code; set the quantity (price can be negotiated per quote), then save. Open a quote to view/print its document; "Convert to Order" turns an accepted quote into an order.'],
        ['Orders', '/sales/orders', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Orders. "New Order": choose customer, order date, payment type (cash/credit), add products and quantities (prices come from the customer\'s price list), save. A credit order is always allowed: if the customer has credit invoices past their credit days, the form shows them in amber ("Overdue: ...") as a reminder to follow up the payment, but the order still goes ahead. An order is approved into an invoice (numbered NSK/####). Approvers: the GM, a Director, the Warehouse Manager or an Accountant - an accountant never approves an order they raised (Accountant A raises, Accountant B approves), and whoever raised it is not sent the approval alert. From an order you can create the dispatch. Past orders (dated on or before 31/10/2026) do not move stock.'],
        ['Invoices', '/sales/invoices', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Invoices. Invoices are made from approved orders and numbered NSK/#### (from 02/11/2026 the series starts at NSK/0432). Open one to see lines, payments and balance; "View Document" shows the printable invoice with its delivery note, the invoice\'s Dated date and, above it, Invoice Date, the day it was entered in the system (kept compact: totals and VAT are the last rows of the items table, the Scan me QR sits beside the payment details, and it shows no amount paid or balance - those are on the invoice screen and the customer statement; when printed, an invoice that would spill onto a second page is shrunk to fit one page if it stays readable, down to 80%, about 10 lines, and a longer invoice keeps its normal size but is shrunk slightly when that removes a nearly empty last page); row menu > Download gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Supplier and delivery invoices are not here: the Suppliers page has their tabs: "Delivery Invoices" (the transporters DI-##### invoices, paid with their Pay button or from Supplier Payments) and "Supplier Invoices" - what suppliers billed us (bills such as PUR-131, and their debit notes from the filter): date, supplier, total, paid, balance, status (unpaid / part paid / paid) and "Paid By", the supplier payments that paid each (e.g. PV-161). Search finds an invoice by number, supplier, description or the payment that paid it; click a row to see each payment and the amount it put on that invoice. Supplier invoices are made three ways: (1) goods received create one automatically (amount = quantity x buying price plus shipping/logistics and the PO VAT, supplier invoice no. = the receipt reference, due date from the supplier payment terms; it follows the receipt if the receipt is edited); (2) "Record Supplier Invoice" for bills that are not goods (services, rent): supplier, their invoice no., date, due date, amount, VAT, description and optional account code (blank = Inventory); (3) Template then Import (preview first) for many at once, including debit notes (Type = Debit Note). New ones are numbered on from PUR- (debit notes DN-) and can be paid from Supplier Payments. Export gives Excel (same columns as the import, so it can be edited and imported back), PDF or CSV. An unpaid recorded invoice can be deleted from its details; one from goods received changes with its receipt. Every tab shows Total Value, Paid, Unpaid and Number of Invoices at the top, for what the list is showing now: search a transporter or supplier such as Imevo and the four figures become theirs, and filters count too; cancelled invoices are listed but not counted. Every tab pages 20 / 50 / 250 / All rows (Rows per page under the table) and exports Excel/PDF/CSV.'],
        ['Credit Notes', '/sales/credit-notes', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Credit Notes. "New Credit Note": pick the invoice, the items/quantities returned and the Credit Note Date. Issuing it returns the goods to the batches they came from and reduces what the customer owes. To print or download one credit note: in the list, Actions (...) > "Credit Note (Print / Download)", or open it and click "Credit Note (Print / Download)". It is laid out like the debit note: letterhead; our details with the buyer (Bill to: business name, address, PIN) under them; Credit Note No., Dated, Original Invoice No. & Date, Mode/Terms of Payment, Buyer\'s Order No. & Date, Other References (customer phone - contact), Dispatch Doc No., Prepared By, Dispatched through, Destination (these from the invoice it credits), Terms of Delivery, Reason for Credit; Sl No., Description of Goods with Batch and Expiry under the name (where the returned stock went into a batch, else the invoice\'s batches; past sales that never moved stock have none), Quantity, Rate, per, Amount; Less Discount and VAT; Total; amount in words; payment details with the Scan me QR; declaration and Authorised Signatory. Print, or Download as PDF, Excel or CSV.'],
        ['Cheques', '/sales/cheques', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Cheques: post-dated cheques received, their maturity, clearing or bouncing (a bounced cheque re-opens the invoices and adds a KES 2,000 charge). Tabs: "Cheques" and "Bounced Cheque Debit", which lists every bounced customer cheque with its debit from the earliest to date: S/No, Debit No. (DEB-001, DEB-002...; click it to open the document), date, customer, cheque no., bank, cheque amount, debit, paid, balance and status, with search, paging and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV). A debit has its own DEB series and takes no invoice number or NSK number, so it is not on the Filing page. It is not listed in Sales > Invoices, only on this tab. Otherwise it behaves as an invoice: it shows under its DEB number on the customer\'s Account and on their statement and ledger, and receipts pay it in date order with the invoices. A cheque is always accepted, however old the invoices it pays: the customer\'s PD cheque credit period is a recorded term and refuses nothing. It always pays the oldest invoice first, through to the newest.'],
        ['Receipts (customer payments)', '/payments', ['can_view_payments_menu'],
            'Receipts: money received from customers. "Receive Payment": customer, amount, method (cash, M-Pesa, bank, cheque), reference. A customer\'s payment always pays their oldest unpaid invoice first, through to the newest, and nobody picks the invoice or the split: "Record Payment" on any invoice, "Map Payment" and "Allocate to Invoices" all work the same way, and the message says where the money went. Anything left stays as credit on the customer\'s account. Money is never refused (a cheque does the same when approved). Only money taken while creating a sale (a cash sale, down payment or order deposit) stays on that sale. Several payments may quote the same reference (e.g. two receipts for one invoice no.). A product short on stock never stops a receipt: the stock there is taken and the payment goes through. If a payment can\'t be saved, a red message says why and what to do; if it gives a reference, quote it to support. If an invoice was paid more than its total, the extra counts as the customer\'s credit (the Account Balance names the invoice) and "Apply Credit to Invoices" moves it to the open invoices, oldest first, so the balance matches the ledger. The cards at the top: Total Received is completed receipts only (failed and pending never count as money received); Failed Payments shows the sum of failed receipts (e.g. bounced cheques) with how many there are. Cancelling or deleting a receipt takes its money off the invoices it paid, then the customer\'s other receipts are re-sorted so the oldest invoices stay paid first and the newest invoice is the one left owing (the message lists what moved).'],
        ['Dispatch', '/dispatch', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Dispatch: deliveries of orders and requisitions. Create a dispatch from an order, choose transporter/zone (the Dispatch list shows each sale by its NSK invoice number, or ORD until it is invoiced, and search finds either; delivery rates set the cost per carton; the Warehouse Manager or an Accountant adds, edits or deletes a rate on the Delivery Rates tab, and the GM, a Director or an Accountant approves or rejects it, but an accountant never approves a rate they raised or last edited (Accountant A raises, Accountant B approves); editing an approved rate sends it back for approval), approve it, then print the delivery note (two copies: Original and Duplicate). Delivery invoices from transporters are paid under Sales > Invoices > Delivery. A rejected dispatch can always be approved again: open it in Dispatch and click Approve again (anyone with the approve-dispatches permission). It goes back in its approval line with the rejected step pending again, takes the order stock again, and is approved straight away when the person clicking is the next approver; it cannot be reopened while the order has another live dispatch (approve or cancel that one) or when the order is cancelled.'],
        ['Requisitions', '/requisitions', ['can_view_requisitions_menu'],
            '"Create New Requisition", add products, submit for approval; an approved requisition can be dispatched.'],
        ['Products', '/inventory/products', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Inventory > Products. To add a product: click "Add Product", enter the item description, unit of measure, cost price, tax status (VAT 16%/zero rated/exempt), reorder level, and its prices per price list (NSPV, NSPH, NSPO, NSPD, each with its item code), add sizes if it comes in several, then save. Many at once: "Import" with the Excel template (Export gives the same sheet to edit and re-import). Stock is the total of the product\'s batches.'],
        ['Price Lists', '/inventory/price-lists', ['can_create_products'],
            'Inventory > Price Lists: choose a list (NSPV, NSPH, NSPO, NSPD or a new one), "Download current" to edit prices in Excel, upload it, Preview, then Import. Importing NSPV also sets each product\'s default price. A row finds its item by code; if the row\'s description names a different existing item exactly (e.g. NSPV 036 written as "Ceftriaxone 1gm Vial" while the code sat on the 500 mg item), the code moves to that item and the preview says from where. Price lists never rename items: if the description matches no item, the price goes on the code\'s item with a note - rename the product itself (Products > edit). Each list takes only its own codes: an NSPO/NSPH/NSPD code in an NSPV upload (or any other list\'s code) is refused as an error row and nothing is saved for it; writing the list\'s own prefix is fine ("NSPV001" on NSPV is saved as 001). If a list already holds another list\'s codes (an NSPO sheet once uploaded as NSPV), a red box on that list says how many - "Show them" lists them, "Remove N misfiled prices" deletes only those (the other list\'s own prices are untouched), then upload the correct sheet.'],
        ['Batches', '/inventory/batches', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Inventory > Batches: each batch\'s number, expiry, quantity received/available and cost. Stock comes from batches; goods come in through Product Receipts. Only batches with stock are listed and exported; a used-up batch is kept (for returns, recalls and sales history) but hidden - pick "Sold Out (used up)" in the status filter to see them. One item can have many batches with the same batch number (e.g. one number delivered 10 times). To capture batches in Excel: Export > Excel on the Batches page, keep every row, add a row per new batch (Item Description exactly as in the system, Batch Number, Quantity Received, Quantity Available, Received Date, Expiry Date, Unit Cost), then Import Batches, check the Preview and Import. A row with the same Item Description, Batch Number and Received Date as a batch already there updates that batch (the quantity typed replaces it, it is not added); a row with a new Received Date, or an extra row beyond those already there, is a new batch.'],
        ['Product Receipts (goods received)', '/product-receipt', ['can_view_product_receipt_menu'],
            '"Create Receipt": supplier, store, document type and reference (supplier invoice/delivery note no.), then each product with batch number, expiry, quantity and unit cost; import costs (duty, clearing, transport) are spread over the units. Receiving against a purchase order: open the PO and click "Receive goods". Saving a receipt also creates the supplier invoice (Suppliers > Supplier Invoices) at those buying prices, so a line with a buying price of 0 asks for confirmation first. Returning goods to the supplier: on the receipt\'s row, Actions (...) > "Debit Note (Return to Supplier)", or the "Debit Note" button in the receipt\'s details, or Suppliers > Debit Notes > "Create Debit Note" (pick the receipt): enter the quantity going back on each line (up to what was received and not yet returned, and what is left of that receipt\'s own batch - goods already sold cannot go back), the date, the reason and optionally the supplier\'s credit note no. It raises a supplier debit note numbered SDEB/0001, SDEB/0002, ...: the stock comes out of that receipt\'s batch (sent to eTIMS as a supplier return), the amount (buying price x quantity, plus the PO\'s VAT) comes off what is owed on that receipt\'s supplier invoice, and the books post DR the supplier (Payables) / CR Inventory (and Input VAT). It shows in Suppliers > Debit Notes (which lists only these SDEB debit notes, with From Receipt and Recorded By; the DN- ones brought across from Tally are not there but stay in the books, in supplier balances and under Supplier Invoices > Debit notes) and in Suppliers > Supplier Invoices (filter Debit notes), with its items (batch and expiry). Clicking it opens its details: "Debit Note (Print / Download)" opens (in the same window) the printed debit note laid out like the company\'s Tally debit note (letterhead; our name, address, contact and e-mail with the supplier (Debit to) name, address and PIN under it; Debit Note No., Dated, Original Invoice No. & Date (the supplier\'s invoice the goods came in on), Other References (supplier\'s credit note no.), Goods Received No., Prepared By, Reason for Return; Sl No., Description of Goods with Batch and Expiry under the name, Quantity (e.g. 100 PCS), Rate, per, Amount; Total; then, as on the sales invoice, Amount Chargeable in words and E. & O.E, the PAYMENT DETAILS box (Company\'s KRA PIN, M-Pesa paybill and account, bank details) with the Scan me QR code, the Declaration, for Nescopharm Africa Limited / Authorised Signatory) with Print and Download (PDF/Excel/CSV). A supplier\'s PIN prints from the KRA PIN field on the supplier. "Edit" (GM, Director and Accountant to start: permission "Edit Debit Notes" on Users > Roles) changes the date, reason, supplier\'s credit note no. and quantities; it keeps its SDEB number, its old quantities go back into stock and the new ones come out, and the receipt\'s invoice and the books follow. Deleting it puts the stock back and the supplier is owed again. A receipt with a debit note cannot have its items edited or be deleted until the debit note is deleted.'],
        ['Stock Counts', '/inventory/stock-counts', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            '"New Stock Count", count the items, submit; approving it records the variances.'],
        ['Stock Adjustments', '/inventory/stock-adjustments', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Adjust a batch\'s quantity (damage, expiry, correction) with a reason; adjustments are approved before they change stock.'],
        ['Suppliers', '/suppliers', ['can_view_suppliers_menu'],
            'The Suppliers page has tabs: Suppliers, Delivery Invoices and Supplier Invoices. "Add Supplier" with contacts, payment terms, bank details and KRA PIN; Import/Template for many at once. A supplier\'s statement shows bills and payments. Its "Ledger (Double Entry)" box is the supplier\'s ledger account from the books in the Tally layout - Date, Dr/Cr, Particulars (the other side of the entry, e.g. "Cr Bank Accounts" for a payment, "Dr Inventory" for a purchase), Vch Type, Vch No., Debit, Credit - opening with the Opening Balance and ending with Total and the Closing Balance (Cr = we owe them, written in the Debit column as Tally does); choose From/To and Apply, "Show edits & reversals" to see corrected entries, Export for Excel/PDF/CSV. The "General Ledger" button on a supplier (or transporter) opens Finance > General Ledger filtered to them: every journal entry for their bills, delivery invoices, goods received, debit notes and payments, with both sides (e.g. Dr Inventory or Transport / Cr Accounts Payable for a bill; Dr Accounts Payable / Cr Bank for a payment); the Payables account closing balance is what is owed to them.'],
        ['Purchase Orders', '/purchase-orders', ['can_view_purchase_orders_menu'],
            '"New Purchase Order": supplier, store, order date, products with quantities and cost. It is approved ("Approve") by the GM, a Director or an Accountant - an accountant never approves a purchase order they raised (another accountant, the GM or a Director does), and the one who raised it is not sent the approval alert - then goods are received against it ("Receive goods" creates the product receipt). Row menu: View Details, Download, Return to supplier.'],
        ['Supplier Payments', '/supplier-payments', ['can_view_payments_menu'],
            '"Record Supplier Payment": supplier, amount, date, method and reference (and the PO if it is for one). Picking the supplier lists its unpaid invoices under "Invoices Paid" - its bills and, for a transporter, its delivery invoices - oldest first: tick each invoice this payment pays (it fills the full amount owed, which can be lowered for a part payment; the payment amount follows), or type the amount and click "Pay oldest first". One payment (e.g. one cheque) can pay many invoices. Cash, bank and M-Pesa pay the invoices at once; a cheque pays them only when it is marked cleared. The payments list shows each payment\'s invoices (Invoices / PO column, searchable), and View Details lists them with what was paid on each. The invoices themselves are listed under Sales > Invoices > Supplier Invoices. The list pages 20 / 50 / 250 / All rows.'],
        ['Expenses', '/expenses', ['can_view_expenses_menu'],
            '"Add Expense": category, description, amount, date, payment method and receipt; expenses are approved. Categories are managed on the same page. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV.'],
        ['Filing', '/filing', ['can_view_filing'],
            'Every invoice by customer with the File No its printed copy is filed under (NSK number). File numbers can be typed in or filled in the exported Excel and imported back.'],
        ['Finance', '/finance', ['can_view_finance_menu'],
            'Finance > Aging (receivables and payables): receivables use every unpaid invoice in Sales > Invoices, whatever its status, plus unpaid bounced cheque debits (DEB-...), which Sales > Invoices does not list. Collection Summary shows each customer\'s unpaid invoices and debits under their line, and so does its export. Total Outstanding is everything unpaid, due or not, split into Not Yet Due and Overdue. The cards and totals follow the search, which finds a customer by any words of its name or contact, or an invoice number, on both Collection Summary and Invoice Tracker; the Invoice Tracker also shows each customer\'s total outstanding. The page refreshes by itself. The 0-30, 31-60, 61-90 and >90 day columns go by each invoice\'s age from its invoice date (121 days old sits in >90). Days Overdue, Not Yet Due and Overdue go by the due date, which is the invoice date plus the customer\'s Credit Period, whatever the invoice was saved with (121 days old on 60-day terms is 61 days overdue); changing a customer\'s Credit Period moves their invoices with it, a bounced cheque debit stays due on receipt, and a customer with no Credit Period keeps the invoice\'s own terms. Finance > Reports > Income Statement is the full profit and loss for a chosen period: Revenue to Net Revenue, Cost of Sales to Gross Profit, Operating Expenses (Selling & Marketing, General & Administrative, R&D, Non-Cash) to Operating Income / EBIT, Non-Operating items to Earnings Before Taxes, then Taxes to Net Profit. Each ledger account sits on a line by its name and is listed under it; one no line recognises goes on an Other line, so the statement always equals the ledger. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Reports leave out corrections (an entry replaced when its document was edited or removed, and its reversal), so each month shows that month\'s invoices; a bounced cheque stays. Income Statement sales exclude VAT, while the Sales report\'s Sales Accounts show invoice totals including VAT and list only sales from orders, so bounced cheque charges appear only on the statement. An amber note warns when Cost of Sales looks too low or no income tax is recorded. Finance: Chart of Accounts, Journal Entries, General Ledger (per account, filter by date/account/customer/supplier or transporter - a customer or supplier filter shows only their entries with both sides of each; every account opens with an Opening Balance row and ends with a Closing Balance row with total debits and credits, and an account with a balance brought forward but no entries in the period still shows; Download), Trial Balance, Balance Sheet, Income Statement (profit and loss), Cash Flow, Financial Ratios, Aging, Bank Accounts (withdrawals/transfers), Budgets, Tax Rates, Account Mappings.'],
        ['Sales Reports', '/reports/sales', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Sales: pick the period, then tabs Sales Accounts (ledger, with "Show receipts"), Orders, Products, Customers, By Region, By County, By Town, By Sales Rep, Daily Log. Every table has Export (Excel/PDF/CSV).'],
        ['Inventory Reports', '/reports/inventory', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Inventory: Stock Balance, Low and Out of Stock, Stock Movement, Stock Management (closing stock value; Total Value = sales incl. VAT; Cost Value = sold quantity x unit cost; Gross Profit = TV - CV; Profit % = gross profit / cost x 100; reorder alerts). At the end of Stock Management: Gross (all items), Less credit notes (those dated in the period, for these items, at full value: TV, CV and profit), Net (after credit notes); the Gross Profit card shows the net. Stock Management has a Pricing filter (All pricing, NSPD, NSPH, NSPO, NSPV, No price code): it shows only items on that list, and Sold, Total Value, Cost Value, Gross Profit and the credit notes count only sales made on that list code (opening, closing and cover stay the whole stock of the item). The search, category, store and pricing filters drive Gross, Less credit notes and Net too. Click any column heading to sort (again to reverse), e.g. Unit Cost, Total Value, Profit %. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV, with those end rows.'],
        ['CRM Reports > Customer Stats', '/reports/customers?tab=stats', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > CRM Reports (Customer Reports): two tabs. Customer Growth = new customers per day (chart and table). Customer Stats = every customer with S/No, Customer, Customer No., Phone, Orders, Order Value, Invoices, Invoiced, Paid, Balance, Last Order; customers with no orders show 0 and 0.00. Pick From/To dates to count only that period (blank = all time); Search by customer name, number or phone; Show all / with orders / without orders; click a column heading to sort (again to reverse); 20/50/250/All per page; Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV with totals.'],
        ['eTIMS (send an invoice to KRA)', '/etims', ['can_view_invoices', 'can_view_sales_menu'],
            'To send an invoice to KRA eTIMS: open the invoice (Sales > Invoices, or Filing) > its document > "eTIMS Invoice" (or eTIMS > Invoices, click the invoice > eTIMS invoice), then "Send to eTIMS". The page registers the items with KRA, adds any eTIMS stock the sale needs, submits it and updates by itself until KRA signs (usually under a minute). Then it shows KRA\'s own record laid out like KRA\'s eTIMS invoice: KRA logo, Invoice From/To PINs, CU invoice number (SCU ID/number) and date, every line under its KRA item code and tax rate, SCU information with KRA\'s QR code, and the tax summary (16%, 8%, 0%, Ex.); Print or Download PDF, and "View on KRA" opens KRA\'s receipt page. On eTIMS > Invoices, clicking a signed invoice also offers "Download eTIMS receipt", which saves that PDF straight away. If KRA refuses, the reason shows on the page with "Send to eTIMS" to try again. Sending never creates a product: each invoice line goes to KRA under the product it is for (an old line written for a product whose sizes became products of their own is linked to the right one by its name), and a line that matches no single product stops the invoice with "These lines are not linked to a product" - edit the invoice and pick the product. While eTIMS is on the Test environment it never gets in the way of anything: an invoice goes to KRA\'s sandbox only when someone presses Send to eTIMS (marked "Test - not a tax invoice"), nothing is sent by itself, and credit notes, deletions and order date changes go ahead whatever eTIMS says. Only once eTIMS is switched to Live are invoices sent by themselves, a credit note raised on KRA when issued (its invoice must be signed, unless it is dated before go-live), and signed documents locked. eTIMS > Product Codes lists items and their KRA codes (Export to Excel, fill in the Item Class Codes and import it back; the Ref column that ties each row to its product is hidden, so it is not edited, and a new product or a renamed one is matched too). Adding or editing a product has an optional "KRA Item Class Code" with a suggestion from its name ("Use"); left empty, the product goes to KRA under the general code 99010000 until one is set, and changing it registers a registered product again. A product\'s sizes share its KRA code. eTIMS stock follows the system: opening stock at registration, goods received (02), stock adjustments and counts (06 in / 16 out), breakages, damage and expiry (15) and returns to a supplier (12) are sent by themselves once Live, and a sale short of eTIMS stock adds the shortfall first - do not also add stock on the KRA portal; Settings > eTIMS (eTIMS Setup) holds the KRA PIN, branch, DigiTax API key, Test Connection and Enable.'],
        ['Procurement / CRM / Logistics Reports', '/reports', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports menu: Procurement (purchases by supplier), CRM (customers), Logistics (deliveries), eTIMS Compliance (KRA submissions).'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Employees (add employee)', '/hr/employees', ['can_view_employees_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Employees: the staff list (Total/Active Employees at the top; search by name or email; Export for Excel/PDF/CSV). Click an employee to see their details. To add an employee: click "Add New Employee" and fill in Personal Information (Employee Number, Hire Date, First Name, Last Name, Email, Phone, Date of Birth, Gender, National ID), Address Information, Employment Information (Employment Type, Status, Department, Position, Supervisor, Leave Approver, Salary Advance Approver, and Connect User Account to link their login), Financial Information (Payment Frequency, Basic Salary, Bank Name/Account/Branch), Statutory Details (KRA PIN, NSSF Number, SHIF Number, disability exemption, insurance relief) and any allowances or deductions, then click "Save Employee". The new employee is then in the list and in payroll. Row menu: View, Edit Employee, Delete Employee; a leaver is set to Status Terminated with a Termination Date.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Payroll', '/hr/payroll', ['can_view_payroll_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Payroll: "Create Payroll Run" for a month (choose the employees in Employee Selection), review the Payroll Summary (gross, PAYE, NSSF, SHIF, Housing Levy, net), approve it, then open each employee\'s Payslip; Export gives the payroll sheet and the KRA P10. Payroll configuration holds the tax bands.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Leave Management', '/hr/leave-management', ['can_view_leave_management_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Leave Management: all leave requests. "Create Leave Request" records leave for an employee; open a request to approve or reject it. Employees apply for their own leave in the Employee Portal.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Salary Advance', '/hr/salary-advance', ['can_view_salary_advance_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Salary Advance: all advance requests. "Create Salary Advance Request" for an employee; open one to approve or reject it. Employees request their own in the Employee Portal.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Allowances', '/hr/allowances', ['can_view_employees_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Allowances: allowances paid to employees outside payroll, for the month picked: one-time ones and monthly ones set to "Disbursed" on the employee (Type column). "One-time Allowance" records one paid once: employee, allowance (e.g. Field allowance), amount, date paid, taxable. Payroll leaves it out; it shows in the month of its date and can be removed with its bin icon. "ROI for Reps" opens the report. Every allowance paid outside payroll is also an expense: each payment (a one-time allowance on its date, a monthly one on the 1st of each month or its start date) is recorded under Expenses in the "Salaries & Allowances" category, approved, cash, with the employee as payee and "Allowance: <name>" as description, and posts to the books. It follows the allowance: changing the amount or dates updates those expenses, removing the allowance removes them, and each new month adds the monthly payment.'],
        ['Profit (report)', '/reports/profit', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Profit: profit over any date range (Today, This week, This month or From/To). Total Value (TV) is each invoice line as billed, after discount, including VAT (a month\'s TV equals its Sales Accounts total; negotiated prices count as invoiced, e.g. NSPH 067 listed at 67 but sold at 45 counts 45). Cost Value (CV) is what the goods cost in the batches they left from on the order\'s dispatch; else the item\'s average batch cost; else its Cost Price (sales before 01/11/2026 never moved stock, so they always use these). Profit = TV - CV; Profit % = profit / CV x 100. A line with no cost recorded anywhere counts at cost 0 and is flagged ("No cost") - set the item\'s Cost Price to correct it. Three views: By Price List (NSPH, NSPO, NSPD, NSPV, "No price code"), By Product, Invoice Lines (NSK no., date, customer, item, price code, qty, per, rate, TV, CV, profit, profit %, cost from). Every view ends with Gross, Less credit notes (all credit notes dated in the range, at their full value as on Sales Accounts, minus the cost of the goods back) and Net (after credit notes); the cards show net. Filters: price list, cost from, search; sortable; totals and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV) follow the filters.'],
        ['ROI for Reps (report)', '/reports/rep-roi', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > ROI for Reps: each sales rep\'s allowances (one-time on their date, monthly disbursed once a month) against the invoices raised under the rep (the invoice\'s sales rep, else its order\'s; net of credit notes; drafts and cancelled left out), over a date range (Range: Today, This week, This month, Last 90 days, or From/To for any day, week, month or range). ROI = invoiced / allowance (KES invoiced per KES 1 paid): green 10x and over, amber 1-10x, red under 1x. One table, one row per rep: click a column (Rep, Allowance, Invoices, Invoiced, ROI) to sort; filter by ROI band or search a rep; rows per page 20/50/250/All; totals and Export follow the filters. For a rep\'s invoices to count: their user\'s role must be a sales rep role, the order/invoice must name them as sales rep, and their employee record must be linked to that user (else the row says no user account linked).'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Time Management', '/hr/time-management', ['can_view_time_management_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Time Management: staff time entries. "Create Time Entry" to record one; row menu to view, edit or delete.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Daily Reports (review staff daily work reports)', '/hr/daily-reports', ['can_view_daily_reports_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Daily Reports: the daily work reports staff submitted from the Employee Portal - Date, Employee, Position, Approver, Status (pending/approved/rejected). Filter by Date and by Status; row menu > View Details shows the Work / Activity Log, Key Achievements and Pending Work / Challenges.',
            ['can_approve_daily_reports' => 'Only Directors approve daily reports: every staff report, the GM\'s included, goes to the Directors (a Director\'s own report is approved automatically). A Director opens a pending report: row menu or View Details > Approve (optional Approval Remarks) or Reject (give the Rejection Reason). Anyone else sees "Awaiting approval by a Director" and no Approve/Reject.']],
        ['Employee Portal (my leave, salary advance, daily work report)', '/employee-portal', ['can_view_employee_portal_menu'],
            'Employee Portal: your own HR self-service. The top shows your Employment Summary (Employee Number, Department, Position, Basic Salary) and Approval Routing (Leave, Salary Advance and Daily Reports all go to any Director; a Director\'s own leave or advance goes to any other Director, and a Director\'s own daily report is self-certified). Tabs: Leave - "Apply for Leave" (see days left per leave type), then "Submit Leave Request"; Salary Advance - enter Amount, Request Date and Reason, then "Submit Salary Advance Request"; Daily Reports - the "Submit Daily Work Report" form is open right on the tab (there is no "New Report" button); to write today\'s report: pick the Date, fill the Work / Activity Log for each time slot, Today\'s Key Achievements and Pending Work / Challenges; what you type is saved automatically as you go ("Save Draft" saves it at once), and "Submit Daily Work Report" appears once every part is filled. Daily reports close at 12 midnight: anyone who has not sent the day\'s report gets a bell reminder at 8:00 PM and 10:00 PM, and at 12 midnight whatever they had written is sent to the Directors as it stands, marked "sent at midnight" (this runs on the server, so the page need not be open; Sundays and days on approved leave need no report). Your own reports are listed beside the form under Recent Daily Reports (filter by date) with their status; a pending one can be edited and resubmitted there. Daily reports go to the Directors, who approve or reject them on their own screen. Directors see a Pending Approvals panel here for leave, salary advances and daily reports. The GM and the Directors also see a Staff Activity panel: for any day, who has and has not sent their daily report (and who is still writing one), plus everyone\'s latest daily reports, leave requests and salary advances.'],
        ['Assets (asset register)', '/assets', ['can_view_assets_menu', 'can_view_assets'],
            'Assets Register: the top shows Total Assets, Total Quantity, Total Value and Categories. The table lists each asset: Asset Name/Description, Category, Serial/Model No., Location/Department, Qty and Purchase Cost (Ksh). Search with "Search assets...", choose a category in "Filter by category", click a column heading to sort, and click a row (or row menu > View) to see its details and notes. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV. The register records cost and location; it does not calculate depreciation.',
            ['can_create_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'To add one: "Add Asset", fill Asset Name / Description (required), Category, Serial / Model Number, Location/Department, Quantity (Pcs), Purchase Cost (Ksh) and Notes, then save. Many at once: "Template" downloads the Excel sheet; fill it and click "Import", check the Import Preview (rows with errors are skipped), then confirm.',
             'can_update_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'Row menu > Edit changes an asset.',
             'can_delete_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'Row menu > Delete removes one.']],
        ['SOPs (procedures, temperature records, cleaning records)', '/sops', ['can_view_sops_menu', 'can_view_sops'],
            'SOPs has three tabs, each table with search/filters, sortable columns, paging and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV). SOPs: the procedures with year, status, annexures and comments; "New SOP" adds one. Temperature Records: "Log Today\'s Reading" per thermometer - morning reading before 12:00, afternoon from 12:00 (today only; any past date can be picked to type in an old chart), acceptance max (a reading above it is flagged Out of range). "Checked By" is picked from the people whose role has "Check SOP Records" (GM, Director and Accountant to start with) and they get a notification; "Recorded By" is whoever typed it in. Cleaning Records (the cleaning services sheet): "Record Cleaning" with date, start/end time, areas cleaned (e.g. All areas, Offices/windows, Holiday), cleaned by, and "Checked By" (required) - "Record & Send to Checker" notifies that person. The checker sees "Waiting for your check" at the top, clicks "Check", writes a comment and confirms; whoever prepared it is notified. Each record keeps who prepared it and who checked it. Past sheets for both tabs: "Template", fill it in Excel, "Import" (preview first) - a cleaning row with Checked By filled comes in already checked.',
            ['can_manage_company' => 'Only the GM or a Director can delete temperature or cleaning records, or edit a cleaning record after it has been checked.']],
        ['Users & Roles', '/users', ['can_manage_users_and_roles'],
            'Users: add a user with their email and role; they get an email with a link to set their password, which works for 24 hours (after that they use "Forgot password" on the login page). A role\'s permissions decide which menus and data they see. To change someone\'s role: on the Users tab open the row\'s Actions (...) menu and use "Assign Role" (or "Edit User" > Role). On the Roles tab a role\'s permissions are ticked on or off - e.g. "Check SOP Records" decides who can be picked as the checker of temperature and cleaning records. Anyone who forgot their password uses "Forgot password" on the login page: the emailed link sets a new one and signs out their old sessions. Capitals in an email address do not matter.'],
    ];

    protected static function open(callable $can): \Illuminate\Support\Collection
    {
        return collect(self::AREAS)->filter(fn ($a) => collect($a[2])->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)))->values();
    }

    /** Names and paths of the areas this user can open (the steps come from howTo, to keep prompts small). */
    public static function areaList(callable $can): string
    {
        return self::open($can)->map(fn ($a) => '[' . trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0])) . "]({$a[1]})")->implode('; ');
    }

    /** An area's guide text for this user: the shared steps plus the ones their role allows. */
    protected static function guide(callable $can, array $area): string
    {
        $allowed = collect($area[4] ?? [])
            ->filter(fn ($text, $perms) => collect(explode('|', $perms))->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)));
        $denied = count($area[4] ?? []) - $allowed->count();

        return trim($area[3] . ' ' . $allowed->implode(' '))
            . ($denied ? ' (This user\'s role cannot do the other actions here, such as adding, changing or approving; an administrator or approver does those.)' : '');
    }

    /** The guide text of the open areas that best match a question, e.g. "add product". */
    public static function howTo(callable $can, string $topic): array
    {
        $words = collect(preg_split('/\W+/', strtolower($topic)))->filter(fn ($w) => strlen($w) > 2)
            ->map(fn ($w) => rtrim($w, 's'));
        $scored = self::open($can)->map(function ($a) use ($words, $can) {
            $name = strtolower($a[0]);
            $text = strtolower($a[3] . ' ' . implode(' ', $a[4] ?? []));
            $score = $words->sum(fn ($w) => (str_contains($name, $w) ? 3 : 0) + (str_contains($text, $w) ? 1 : 0));
            // The screen's name without the hint in brackets: "[HR & Payroll > Employees](/hr/employees)".
            $label = trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0]));
            return ['score' => $score, 'area' => $a[0], 'link' => "[{$label}]({$a[1]})", 'how' => self::guide($can, $a)];
        })->filter(fn ($a) => $a['score'] > 0)->sortByDesc('score')->take(3)->values();

        return $scored->isEmpty()
            ? ['note' => 'No matching area the user can open. Areas: ' . self::areaList($can)]
            : ['areas' => $scored->map(fn ($a) => collect($a)->except('score'))->all()];
    }

    /** The path of the screen a text names ("Inventory Reports > Stock Balance" is /reports/inventory), the most specific name first. */
    public static function pathFor(string $text): ?string
    {
        $text = strtolower(str_replace(['→', '›'], '>', $text));
        return collect(self::AREAS)
            ->map(fn ($a) => ['label' => strtolower(trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0]))), 'path' => $a[1]])
            ->filter(fn ($a) => str_contains($text, $a['label']))
            ->sortByDesc(fn ($a) => strlen($a['label']))
            ->first()['path'] ?? null;
    }

    /** Area names this user cannot open, so the assistant can say so instead of guessing. */
    public static function closedAreas(callable $can): array
    {
        return collect(self::AREAS)
            ->reject(fn ($a) => collect($a[2])->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)))
            ->map(fn ($a) => trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0])))->all();
    }
}
